---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import { SITE } from "../config";

const effective = "September 10, 2026";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Privacy Policy"
  description={`How ${SITE.name} handles your data, which cookies it sets, and how to opt out of personalized advertising.`}
>
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-6 sm:px-8 py-10 sm:py-14">
    <h1 class="font-serif text-3xl tracking-tight sm:text-4xl">
      Privacy Policy
    </h1>
    <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-ink-faint">Last updated {effective}</p>

    <div class="prose mt-8">
      <p>
        This policy explains what information {SITE.name} ({SITE.url}) collects,
        why, and what you can do about it. It applies to this website only.
      </p>

      <h2>The short version</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>You can read every page here without giving me anything.</li>
        <li>
          I do not sell personal information, and I never will.
        </li>
        <li>
          Advertising and analytics on this site are provided by third parties
          that set their own cookies. Those are described below, along with how
          to turn them off.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Information collected automatically</h2>
      <p>
        Like almost every website, this one and the services it uses record
        technical details when you visit: your IP address, browser and device
        type, operating system, the pages you viewed, how long you stayed, and
        the page that referred you. This is used to understand which articles
        are useful and to keep the site working. It is not used to identify you
        personally.
      </p>

      <h2>Information you give me</h2>
      <p>
        If you email the address on the <a href="/contact">contact page</a>, I
        receive your address and whatever you write. I keep it only as long as
        needed to reply and to keep a record of corrections, and I do not add it
        to any mailing list without you asking.
      </p>

      <h2>Cookies</h2>
      <p>
        A cookie is a small file a website stores in your browser. This site uses
        them for three purposes: keeping the site functioning, understanding
        aggregate traffic, and serving advertising. You can delete or block
        cookies at any time in your browser settings. Blocking them will not stop
        you reading anything here.
      </p>

      <h2>Your course progress</h2>
      <p>
        If you read the <a href="/start-here">guided course</a>, this site
        remembers which steps you have opened so it can show you where to pick
        up next. That record is a short list of page names kept in your own
        browser's local storage. It is not a cookie, it is never sent to me or
        to anyone else, and it is not linked to any account, because there are
        no accounts here.
      </p>
      <p>
        It stays on the device you used, so it will not follow you to your
        phone. To erase it, use the "Forget my progress" link on the course
        card, or clear this site's data in your browser. If your browser blocks
        local storage, nothing is stored and every page still works normally.
      </p>

      <h2>Advertising</h2>
      <p>
        This site displays advertising served by Google, which uses cookies to
        show ads based on your prior visits to this and other websites.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Google's use of advertising cookies enables it and its partners to
          serve ads to you based on your visit to this site and other sites on
          the internet.
        </li>
        <li>
          You may opt out of personalized advertising by visiting
          <a
            href="https://www.google.com/settings/ads"
            rel="nofollow noopener">Google Ads Settings</a
          >.
        </li>
        <li>
          You can opt out of personalized advertising from many other vendors at
          <a href="https://www.aboutads.info/" rel="nofollow noopener"
            >aboutads.info</a
          > and
          <a href="https://optout.networkadvertising.org/" rel="nofollow noopener"
            >optout.networkadvertising.org</a
          >.
        </li>
        <li>
          Third-party vendors and ad networks other than Google may also serve
          ads here and may use their own cookies to measure the effectiveness of
          those ads.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        How Google handles data from sites that use its services is described in
        <a
          href="https://policies.google.com/technologies/partner-sites"
          rel="nofollow noopener">Google's partner sites policy</a
        >.
      </p>

      <h2>Analytics</h2>
      <p>
        This site uses Google Analytics to measure traffic in aggregate — which
        pages get read, on what kind of device, from which country. IP addresses
        are anonymized. You can prevent Google Analytics from collecting data
        with the
        <a
          href="https://tools.google.com/dlpage/gaoptout"
          rel="nofollow noopener">official browser opt-out add-on</a
        >.
      </p>

      <h2>If you are in the EEA, the UK, or Switzerland</h2>
      <p>
        Where the GDPR applies, the lawful basis for analytics and personalized
        advertising is your consent, collected through the consent banner shown
        on your first visit, and you may withdraw it at any time using that same
        banner. Strictly necessary processing relies on legitimate interest. You
        have the right to request access to your personal data, correction,
        erasure, restriction, portability, and to object to processing. Write to
        the address on the <a href="/contact">contact page</a> to exercise any of
        these.
      </p>

      <h2>If you are in California</h2>
      <p>
        Under the CCPA and CPRA you have the right to know what personal
        information is collected, to request its deletion, to correct it, and to
        opt out of its sale or sharing. {SITE.name} does not sell personal
        information. To opt out of personalized advertising, use the Google Ads
        Settings link above or the consent banner. Requests go to the contact
        address and will not result in any different level of service.
      </p>

      <h2>Children</h2>
      <p>
        This site is written for adults and is not directed at children under 13.
        I do not knowingly collect personal information from children. If you
        believe a child has provided information here, email me and it will be
        deleted.
      </p>

      <h2>External links</h2>
      <p>
        Articles here link out to pattern designers, retailers, and reference
        sources. Once you follow a link you are on someone else's site under
        their privacy policy, not this one.
      </p>

      <h2>Changes</h2>
      <p>
        If this policy changes, the date at the top changes with it. Material
        changes will be noted on the home page.
      </p>

      <h2>Contact</h2>
      <p>
        Questions about this policy, and any request to exercise the rights
        above, go through the <a href="/contact">contact page</a>.
      </p>

      <div class="warning">
        <p>
          <strong>Note for the site owner:</strong> this is a solid, standards-based
          starting policy that covers what Google AdSense requires you to
          disclose. It is not legal advice. Before you launch, confirm the
          analytics and ad vendors listed here match what you actually run, and
          fill in a business address if you register one.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
